<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Basavakalyan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kalyani (Karnataka)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Basavakalyan, historically Kalyani, is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a> district, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. It was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire</a> from the mid-eleventh century and later of the Kalachuris of Kalyani, and in the twelfth century it was the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingayatism">Lingayat</a> movement led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basava">Basava</a>, after whom it was renamed. Its fort and ruins survive.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/basavakalyan.jpg" alt="Basavakalyan" width="320" height="362" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sscheral · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basava_Gaint_Statue_108_feet,_Basava_Kalyana.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before 1956 the town was called Kalyani. After the states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> were reorganised on linguistic lines in that year, it was renamed Basavakalyan in memory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basava">Basava</a>, the social reformer. Over the centuries it was ruled by the Western Chalukyas, the Kalachuris of Kalyani, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seuna-yadava-dynasty">Yadavas of Devagiri</a>, the Kakatiyas, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>, the Bahamani, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a> sultanates, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> and the Nizams of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/someshvara-i">Someshvara I</a> moved the Western Chalukya capital to Kalyani from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manyakheta">Manyakheta</a>, now Malkhed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalaburagi">Kalaburagi</a> district. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-chalukya-empire">Kalyani Chalukyas</a>, so named to distinguish them from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badami">Badami</a>, ruled from the town until 1195. Their king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramaditya-vi">Vikramaditya VI</a> had scholars at his court, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilhana">Bilhana</a>, the poet from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, and Vigyaneshwara, a legal expert. The Kalyani style of temple architecture reached its maturity in the twelfth century, and nearly a hundred monuments of the period are scattered across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>.</p><p>The Kalachuris of Kalyani succeeded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> and kept the town as their capital. In the twelfth century King Bijjala, who took the throne in 1156, appointed Basaveshwara as his prime minister. Basaveshwara led a social movement against untouchability and gender discrimination and encouraged the vachana literature, in which more than 600 people became writers known as Vachanakaras. Tradition holds that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basava">Basava</a> founded the Anubhava Mantapa, a hall of spiritual discussion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Basavakalyan is a city and municipal council in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a> district, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, at an average elevation of 621 metres. The 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 69,717 inhabitants, most of whom speak <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a>. Its fort was built by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> and later held by the Nizams, and a museum holds Jain idols of the tenth and eleventh centuries. Chalukya temples stand at nearby villages such as Narayanpur, and a 108-foot statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basava">Basava</a> stands on a hillock called Sharana Shaila.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Basavakalyan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bijapur compensated by expanding south, but after Muhammad's death in 1656 the succession of the young Ali Adil Shah II prompted a Mughal invasion under Prince Aurangzeb, who took Bidar and <b>Kalyani</b> in 1657.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bijapur-sultanate">Bijapur Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Its name outlived it: the Eastern Chalukyas ruled Vengi until the eleventh century, and the Western Chalukyas of <b>Kalyani</b>, who rose in 973, claimed descent from the house of Vatapi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chalukyas-of-badami">Chalukyas of Badami · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Western Chalukya Empire, also called the empire of the Chalukyas of <b>Kalyani</b> or the Later Chalukyas, was founded in 973 by Tailapa II, a Rashtrakuta feudatory who governed lands around Tardavadi in the Bijapur region.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>As Chalukya power weakened, their chief Bijjala II, a commander and minister of the Chalukya king Taila III, took control of the capital <b>Kalyani</b> around 1156 and by 1162 proclaimed himself emperor.</blockquote><a href="/state/kalachuris-of-kalyani">Kalachuris of Kalyani</a></li></ul></section>
